    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "In response to the global outbreak of COVID-19, many governments implemented widespread stay-at-home orders in an attempt to reduce the number of infections. Many COVID-19 policies forced schools to shift to a virtual learning environment, resulting in numerous adverse effects. I created a dataset that combines school closures and The National Assessment of Educational Progress (NAEP) scores at the district level and by grade. I use this data to understand the characteristics of specific variables and develop an event-study model that measures the effects of various learning modes and other factors related to pandemic education to better understand the outcomes of COVID-19 policies. Failure rates in math and reading for grades four and eight increased after the pandemic. Children from disadvantaged economic backgrounds are disproportionately affected by the closures. Math scores, particularly among younger students, are most affected by the mode of schooling and other pandemic-related factors.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Angel, B. (2024). COVID-19 School Closures: The Effects on Students Measured by Standardized Testing Scores. Stanford Digital Repository. Available at https://purl.stanford.edu/yz629rd8464. https://doi.org/10.25740/yz629rd8464.",
            "type": "preferred citation",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        }
